   EVALUATION},
Journal = {APPLIED ECOLOGY AND ENVIRONMENTAL RESEARCH},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {24},
Number = {3},
Pages = {5483-5503},
Abstract = {Ecological compensation is a critical mechanism for promoting
   environmental sustainability by encouraging responsible corporate
   behavior. However, existing research lacks practical methods for precise
   ecological compensation accounting, especially in the integration of
   multimodal data and AI technologies. This study proposes a multimodal
   AI-driven precision accounting framework for ecological compensation
   over a five-year observation window (2020-2024), comprising a
   pre-implementation baseline year (2020) and a four-year
   post-implementation period (2021-2024), integrating diverse data
   sources, including remote sensing, socio-economic data, and
   environmental monitoring. The framework utilizes deep neural networks
   (DNN) and graph neural networks (GNN) to construct a dynamic accounting
   model. The framework is validated across several regions in China,
   including the Yangtze River Delta, Pearl River Delta, and parts of
   central and western China. The results show that: (1) the framework
   effectively quantifies compensation activity intensity (CAI) and
   environmental quality changes, with stable and reliable model
   validation; (2) ecological benefits are significantly correlated with
   environmental quality improvements, with NDVI increases of approximately
   12\% in the Yangtze River Delta, 8\% in the Pearl River Delta, and 15\%
   in the central and western regions; (3) the study provides a
   comprehensive analysis of ecological and environmental impacts, offering
   precise methodologies for assessing the ecological outcomes of
   compensation mechanisms. This research offers valuable tools for
   transitioning ecological compensation from theoretical evaluation to
   accurate environmental performance measurement.},
