Journal = {ECOLOGICAL INFORMATICS},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {92},
Month = {DEC},
Abstract = {Accurate prediction and forecasting of chlorophyll-a (Chl-a)
   concentrations are crucial for the sustainable management of marine
   ecosystems. While previous research has primarily focused on
   reconstructing and analyzing historical Chl-a data, limited efforts have
   been made to develop robust predictive models. The present study
   addresses this gap by applying machine learning (ML) techniques to
   predict and forecast Chl-a concentrations in the northern Indian Ocean
   (NIO) using satellite-derived time series data from 1998 to 2022. Key
   environmental variables, including sea surface temperature (SST), sea
   surface height (SSH), and mixed layer depth (MLD), were integrated into
   the analysis due to their strong influence on primary productivity. Five
   ML models, XGBoost, long short-term memory (LSTM), Random Forest (RF),
   multi-layer perceptron (MLP), and support vector regression (SVR) were
   evaluated for their ability to handle complex temporal patterns and
   nonlinear interactions. Model performance was assessed using root mean
   squared error (RMSE), mean absolute error (MAE), mean absolute
   percentage error (MAPE), and the coefficient of determination (R2).
   Among these models, XGBoost achieved the best performance, with an RMSE
   of 0.056, R2 of 0.830, and MAPE of 0.078. LSTM, RF, and MLP also
   produced competitive results, whereas SVR was less effective due to its
   limitations in capturing data complexity. Forecasted Chl-a values
   revealed a declining trend in primary productivity, suggesting potential
   ecological shifts in the NIO. Overall, this study highlights the
   effectiveness of XGBoost for forecasting Chl-a using satellite data and
   demonstrate its potential as a scalable framework to inform climate
   change assessments, guide marine ecosystem monitoring, and support
   sustainable resource management.},
